Note = {27th International Conference on Human-Computer Interaction, HCII-HCII,
   Goeteborg, SWEDEN, JUN 22-27, 2025},
Abstract = {In recent years, desktop 3D printers have become popular due to their
   affordability and compact size, being used by designers for prototypes,
   students for models, and families for DIY projects. As 3D printing
   technology becomes more accessible, it has led to the development of
   localized community ``FabStores{''} and promoted distributed
   manufacturing. Some researchers have suggested a distributed recycling
   system using 3D printing to process plastic waste within communities,
   reducing transportation-related losses. While 3D printing is often
   viewed as environmentally friendly, its widespread use generates
   significant transportation costs, including packaging and emissions.
   Additionally, the low entry barrier has increased consumer demand,
   leading to frequent user errors, unsatisfactory prints, and waste ending
   up in landfills. Many 3D printing users have recognized this issue and
   conducted research to address it. This paper explores 3D printing waste
   and examines how to transition toward a more environmentally sustainable
   3D printing service system, including the development of a distributed
   waste recycling system through literature research, case studies, and
   internet data analysis.},
