Abstract = {This study presents a novel approach to fabricate 3D-printed
   phosphorous-based acrylate monomers using stereolithographic 3D printing
   technology, aiming to create demonstrators with exceptional shape-memory
   characteristics and robust flame retardant properties. The synthesized
   materials combine the advantages of 3D printing precision with the
   intrinsic flame retardancy of phosphorous-based monomers, offering a
   versatile solution for applications requiring both shape memory
   functionality and fire resistance. The limiting oxygen index (LOI) value
   of the intrinsically flame-retardant shape-memory polymer (IFR-SMP) can
   reach 34\%, and the vertical combustion rating after UL 94 can obtain
   V-0. The mechanism of the IFR-SMP flame retardant mainly includes
   terminating a free-radical chain reaction and gas phase dilution, which
   indicates that the gas-phase mechanism plays an important role in the
   flame retardancy. This work advances the frontiers of 3D printing
   technology by demonstrating the synergistic potential of shape memory
   and flame retardancy within a single material system, providing a
   pathway toward the development of innovative and resilient materials for
   the future.
   Stereolithographic 3D printing of phosphorous-based acrylate monomers
   enabling demonstrators with exceptional shape-memory and flame
   retardancy, is presented. The vertical combustion rating of V-0 and
   limiting oxygen index (LOI) of 34\% resulted a flame-induced
   shape-memory effect (SME) in the samples. This synergistic effect of
   shape-memory and flame retardancy can be helpful to develop resilient
   materials useful for fire safety requirements. image},
