Year = {2024},
Volume = {121},
Pages = {323-332},
Month = {JUL 15},
Abstract = {The flexural behavior of sandwich structures produced through the foam
   additive manufacturing (FAM) process was examined. FAM allows the
   creation of intricate foamed structures with varying morphology and
   density gradients using a physical blowing agent (PBA). This technology
   facilitates the production of complex, engineered parts made from a
   single material intentionally designed with different densities and
   mechanical properties in various sections, as well as enabling gradient
   structures. As foams are widely used in industrial applications for
   composite sandwich structures, the study aims to investigate the
   mechanical behavior under three-points loading of 3D printed foam
   sandwich structures produced with various printing parameters, resulting
   in distinct densities and mechanical properties. The failure modalities
   were analyzed by infrared thermography and microscopy. The results
   highlight the influence of printing parameters on the foam's
   characteristics. The bending tests provide insights into the foam's
   strength and energy absorption capabilities. Gibson-Ashby curve was
   built to provide the previsional mechanical strength of the foamed
   sandwich structures, based on their core shape and density. These
   findings contribute to optimizing FAM parameters for crafting
   lightweight, durable components with customized mechanical properties,
   achieved using monomaterial structures. This approach makes recycling
   easier and less impactful, benefiting industries such as aviation and
   automotive.},
